The wind on the Isle of Solitude did not merely blow; it screamed. It tore at the fraying edges of Elias’s coat and rattled the iron bars of the lighthouse window, a constant, rhythmic reminder of his isolation. For thirty years, Elias had been the keeper of the Black Rock Light, a solitary sentinel against the churning gray sea. His world was defined by the beam’s rotation, the creak of the gears, and the salt-crusted silence that settled over the island like a heavy blanket.

Elias was a man of routine. He polished the lens, he trimmed the wick, he logged the weather. He had not spoken to another human being in four months, save for the supply boat that arrived once a month, dropping crates of coal and canned goods before vanishing into the mist. He had convinced himself that solitude was not a punishment, but a choice. A necessary trade for the peace of mind that came with being the only thing standing between the ships and the jagged rocks.

Then, the letter arrived.

